.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH dedens 1  "dedens " 
.SH NAME
dedens \- A dig\-like DNS client
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdedens\fR [\fB\-\-server\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-tls\-ca\fR] [\fB\-\-tls\-insecure\fR] [\fB\-x \fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-wire\fR] [\fB\-\-repeat\fR] [\fB\-\-trace\fR] [\fB\-\-search\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-\-tcp\fR] [\fB\-\-tls\fR] [\fB\-\-quic\fR] [\fB\-\-https\fR] [\fB\-\-https\-get\fR] [\fB\-\-https\-post\fR] [\fB\-\-odoh\-proxy\fR] [\fB\-\-coap\fR] [\fB\-\-doc\-discover\fR] [\fB\-\-dnscrypt\-cert\fR] [\fB\-\-dnscrypt\-relay\fR] [\fB\-\-odoh\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-ignore\fR] [\fB\-\-recurse\fR] [\fB\-\-cdflag\fR] [\fB\-\-adflag\fR] [\fB\-\-edns\fR] [\fB\-\-bufsize\fR] [\fB\-\-dnssec\fR] [\fB\-\-nsid\fR] [\fB\-\-cookie\fR] [\fB\-\-time\fR] [\fB\-\-tries\fR] [\fB\-\-validate\fR] [\fB\-\-trust\-anchor\fR] [\fB\-\-insecure\fR] [\fB\-\-format\fR] [\fB\-\-short\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-yaml\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME|TYPE|CLASS\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
A dig\-like DNS client.
.PP
dig\*(Aqs syntax works too: `@server` is `\-\-server server`, `+opt` is `\-\-opt`, `+noopt` is `\-\-opt=false` and `+opt=value` is `\-\-opt=value`, `opt` any unique prefix of a long flag; `\-t TYPE`, `\-c CLASS` and `\-q NAME` are words.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-server\fR \fI<SERVER>\fR
A server: an address, with or without a port, or a host name; `tls://`, `quic://` or `https://HOST/PATH` before it for DNS over TLS, QUIC or HTTPS, `coap://HOST/PATH` for DNS over CoAP, `sdns://…` for the `DNSCrypt` resolver of a DNS stamp (a build with the `dnscrypt` feature). Each query is asked of each, side by side. Default: the first `nameserver` of /etc/resolv.conf
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
The port of a server given without one, a stamp having its own. Default: 443 over HTTPS, 5683 over CoAP, 853 over TLS or QUIC, else 53
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-ca\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Trust the certificate authorities of this PEM file over TLS, instead of the system\*(Aqs
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-insecure\fR[=\fI<TLS_INSECURE>\fR] [default: false]
Accept any server certificate over TLS, unverified: the connection is encrypted, the server unknown
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-x\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Ask PTR for the reverse name of an address (RFC 1035 §3.5, RFC 3596 §2.5)
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Run the queries of a query file, YAML or JSON, `\-` for stdin, and check their expectations
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Run only the queries of the file with this id
.TP
\fB\-\-save\-wire\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
Write each response to `DIR/<id>.bin`, as received
.TP
\fB\-\-repeat\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 1]
Ask each query this many times in a row, then print its last response and the spread of round trips
.TP
\fB\-\-trace\fR[=\fI<TRACE>\fR] [default: false]
Follow each query from the root servers down the referrals, printing every response, as dig
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR[=\fI<SEARCH>\fR] [default: false]
Try a name under the search list of /etc/resolv.conf until one is not NXDOMAIN, as dig
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print what the exchanges do on stderr: `\-v` dropped messages, retries and fallbacks, `\-vv` every message too
.TP
\fB\-\-tcp\fR[=\fI<TCP>\fR]
TCP from the start. Default: UDP, then TCP on a truncated response (RFC 7766 §5)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\fR[=\fI<TLS>\fR]
DNS over TLS (RFC 7858), its server on port 853 unless given another; over `tcp`. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-quic\fR[=\fI<QUIC>\fR]
DNS over QUIC (RFC 9250), its server on UDP port 853 unless given another; over `tls` and `tcp`. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-https\fR[=\fI<PATH>\fR]
DNS over HTTPS (RFC 8484) over HTTP/3, its server on UDP port 443 unless given another: true, false, or the path of its URI, which `https://HOST/PATH` as a server overrides. Default path: /dns\-query. Not with `tcp`, `tls` or `quic`. Default: off
.TP
\fB\-\-https\-get\fR[=\fI<HTTPS_GET>\fR]
DNS over HTTPS with the query in the URI of a GET, as `https` does by default. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-https\-post\fR[=\fI<HTTPS_POST>\fR]
DNS over HTTPS with the query in the body of a POST. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-odoh\-proxy\fR \fI<URI>\fR
Oblivious DNS over HTTPS (RFC 9230) over HTTP/3 through this proxy, an `https://HOST[:PORT][/PATH]` URI, to the server, which must be an `https://` one: the target. Built with the `odoh` feature only (D69). Not with `tcp`, `tls`, `quic` or `https_get`. Default: off
.TP
\fB\-\-coap\fR[=\fI<COAP>\fR]
DNS over CoAP (RFC 9953), unprotected, over UDP, its server on port 5683 unless given another: the `doc` feature of the build. Not with `tcp`, `tls`, `quic`, `https` or `odoh_proxy`. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-doc\-discover\fR[=\fI<DOC_DISCOVER>\fR]
Ask the CoAP server for its `DoC` resource, GET `/.well\-known/core?rt=core.dns`, and query the first it lists, instead of the path of the URI (RFC 9953 §3.1; D95). The server is the one named, never found: the list is as trusted as the server is. Needs CoAP (`coap`, or a `coap://` server) with no path in the server\*(Aqs URI. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-dnscrypt\-cert\fR[=\fI<DNSCRYPT_CERT>\fR]
List the certificates of a `DNSCrypt` resolver, the server, which must be an `sdns://` stamp (D98): their system, serial and validity, instead of an answer. Not with `tls`, `quic`, `https`, `odoh_proxy` or `coap`. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-dnscrypt\-relay\fR \fI<RELAY>\fR
Ask the `DNSCrypt` resolver, the server, which must be an `sdns://` stamp, through this relay (Anonymized `DNSCrypt`, D100), so that the resolver never sees the client\*(Aqs address: an `sdns://` relay stamp, or an address with an optional port (443). Certificates are asked through it too. Not with `tls`, `quic`, `https`, `odoh_proxy` or `coap`. Default: direct
.TP
\fB\-\-odoh\-config\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
A file with the target\*(Aqs key configurations (RFC 9230 §5), as the target serves them, for `odoh_proxy`: they are not fetched from the target, which would give it the client\*(Aqs address, and no query is linked to it. Read once, before any query: a file that cannot be read or holds no configurations (RFC 9230 §6) is a usage error. Default: fetched from the target
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\fR[=\fI<IGNORE>\fR]
Keep a truncated UDP response instead of asking again over TCP. Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-recurse\fR[=\fI<RECURSE>\fR]
Recursion desired, RD (RFC 1035 §4.1.1). Default: on
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-cdflag\fR[=\fI<CDFLAG>\fR]
Checking disabled, CD (RFC 4035 §3.2.2). Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-adflag\fR[=\fI<ADFLAG>\fR]
Authentic data wanted, AD (RFC 6840 §5.7). Default: on, as dig
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-edns\fR[=\fI<EDNS>\fR]
EDNS (RFC 6891). Default: on
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-bufsize\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The UDP payload size advertised with EDNS. Default: 1232 (D10)
.TP
\fB\-\-dnssec\fR[=\fI<DNSSEC>\fR]
DNSSEC records wanted, DO, with EDNS (RFC 3225 §3). Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-nsid\fR[=\fI<NSID>\fR]
The server\*(Aqs identifier asked, with EDNS (RFC 5001). Default: off
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-cookie\fR[=\fI<COOKIE>\fR]
The cookie, with EDNS (RFC 7873): true, false, or 16 hex digits of a client cookie, then optionally a server cookie. Default: on
.TP
\fB\-\-time\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
How long to wait for each response: seconds, or `500ms`. Default: 5 s
.TP
\fB\-\-tries\fR \fI<N>\fR
Queries sent before giving up when no response comes. Default: 3
.TP
\fB\-\-validate\fR[=\fI<VALIDATE>\fR]
Validate the response as a validating stub resolver (RFC 4035 §4.9): DO and CD set, the chain of trust asked of the same server, a verdict beside the response (D76). The server is the transport, not trusted: its AD flag is never read. Not with `noedns`, `nodnssec` or `nocdflag`. Default: off, unless `trust_anchor` or `insecure` is given
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-trust\-anchor\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
A file of DS or DNSKEY records in zone text, the trust anchors of validation instead of the root\*(Aqs (RFC 4035 §4.4)
.TP
\fB\-\-insecure\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
A negative trust anchor (RFC 7646): names at or below it are insecure, not validated. The command line repeats it
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
The output format
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
human: A status line, the records under their section, the rest in words
.IP \(bu 2
short: The data of the answer records
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
yaml: One YAML document per response
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-short\fR[=\fI<SHORT>\fR] [default: false]
`\-\-format short`
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR[=\fI<JSON>\fR] [default: false]
`\-\-format json`
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-yaml\fR[=\fI<YAML>\fR] [default: false]
`\-\-format yaml`
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
true
.IP \(bu 2
false
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR [default: auto]
Colors: `NO_COLOR` turns them off for `auto`
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fINAME|TYPE|CLASS\fR]
Names, types and classes, as dig reads them: a type or class mnemonic is one, anything else a name. Each name is asked the types after it, else those before the first name, else A; no name asks NS of the root
